## 2. CN Tower

**Location:** Downtown Toronto, Ontario  
**Construction:** February 1973 – April 1975  
**Opened to the public:** 26 June 1976  
**Height:** 553.3 metres (1,815 feet)

### Purpose
Built by Canadian National primarily as a telecommunications tower to restore clear radio and television signals blocked by Toronto’s growing skyline. It also functions as an observation and tourism landmark.

### Significance
- World’s tallest free-standing structure from 1975 until surpassed by the Burj Khalifa (2007–2010)
- Remains the tallest free-standing structure in the Western Hemisphere
- Recognized by the American Society of Civil Engineers as one of the Seven Wonders of the Modern World
- Primary skyline icon and vertical identity marker for Toronto

---

## 3. Space Needle

**Location:** Seattle Center, Seattle, Washington  
**Construction:** Built for the 1962 Century 21 Exposition (World’s Fair)  
**Height:** 605 feet (184 m)

### Purpose and Symbolism
Designed as a lasting landmark for the “Age of Space” theme of the 1962 World’s Fair. It embodies mid-century optimism about technological progress and remains Seattle’s most recognizable skyline element.

